3. COST MODEL
==============================================================================
d=768  F=1152  22 layers = 8 full + 14 sliding (w=64)  head_layers=2

      n      total FLOPs  ratio vs n=1024  encoder q share   head q share
     61       1.0539e+09            0.032            20.4%           1.1%
    512       1.2393e+10            0.377            37.5%           6.5%
   1024       3.2838e+10            1.000            47.9%           9.8%
   2048       9.7889e+10            2.981            58.5%          13.2%
   3006       1.8791e+11            5.722            63.5%          14.8%
   3972       3.0723e+11            9.356            66.7%          15.8%
   4938       4.5522e+11           13.863            68.8%          16.5%
   5946       6.4021e+11           19.496            70.4%          17.0%
   6912       8.4678e+11           25.786            71.6%          17.3%
   8192       1.1646e+12           35.466            72.8%          17.7%

MODELLED  FLOPs(8192)/FLOPs(1024) = 35.466x
MODELLED  FLOPs(7000)/FLOPs(1024) = 26.403x
theoretical closed form: ratio = (a + (2*L_FULL*n0 + (2W+1)*L_LOCAL)*n) / (a + b*n0)
  where a = 22*d^2 + 8*d*F = 20,054,016,  b = 2*L_FULL*D = 12,288
  -> the n^2 terms are 2*L_FULL*n0 = 16384 vs 0.5*(n+n0)*... - the ratio is
     (18190*8192 + 20054016) / (18190*1024 + 20054016)
     = 4.371   (matches the FLOP table)

BOUND: FLOPs(8192)/FLOPs(1024) <= 4.37x for ANY L_full >= 0 in this family.
  the n^2 term of a full layer at n=1024 costs 2*n0*d = 1,572,864 FLOPs against the
  n-independent term 20,054,016 per layer, so at n=1024 the work is mostly linear and the
  ratio behaves like a linear-function ratio, asymptoting to 8192/1024 = 8x from below.

